WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.695 --> 00:00:04.934
[in Ukrainian] People were lying in the fields, swollen

00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:08.145
and so many dead people lay down on the road."

00:00:08.145 --> 00:00:09.723
[in Ukrainian] I survived the Holodomor,

00:00:09.723 --> 00:00:12.184
and will remember it as long as I live.

00:00:12.184 --> 00:00:18.906
This is Rostyslav, reading the words of his
grandmother, Lydia, who lived in Ukraine.

00:00:19.361 --> 00:00:22.757
[in Ukrainian] It was scary to watch and remember. 

00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:26.859
Lydia was a witness and survivor

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:32.290
of a horrific man-made famine that killed
millions in Ukraine.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.551
My father has recorded an oral history that
his grandmother told him

00:00:36.551 --> 00:00:39.051
and he just recorded it in ink pen.

00:00:39.607 --> 00:00:42.682
[in Ukrainian] Our family consisted of 10 people.

00:00:42.682 --> 00:00:46.275
[in Ukrainian] On one of those nights,
my father said that we would starve to death.

00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:48.275
[in Ukrainian] We needed to do something. 

00:00:49.056 --> 00:00:55.036
The famine hit several parts of the 
Soviet Union, from 1932 to 1933.

00:00:55.036 --> 00:00:59.215
But in Ukraine it became known as "the Holodomor"

00:00:59.215 --> 00:01:02.693
a term meaning “death by starvation".

00:01:02.693 --> 00:01:10.136
It was genocide carried out by a dictator
who wanted to keep Ukraine under his control.

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:15.151
And would do everything in his power 
to cover it up for decades.

00:01:26.643 --> 00:01:32.349
In 1917, after the fall of the Russian empire,
Ukraine briefly gained freedom.

00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:38.259
But, by 1922, it was forcefully integrated
into the newly formed Soviet Union

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:43.224
and became known as the 
Ukrainian Socialist Soviet Republic.

00:01:43.224 --> 00:01:46.157
At the time, the country was largely rural

00:01:46.157 --> 00:01:49.784
most of the population were 
villagers and farmers.

00:01:49.784 --> 00:01:53.100
Self-reliance is a very important thing 
for every Ukrainian.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.390
And so having that piece of land, 
cultivating that land

00:01:56.390 --> 00:02:00.116
was something that was very important 
to so many.

00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:03.615
In fact, Ukraine was known for its farmland.

00:02:03.615 --> 00:02:09.015
With some of the world’s most fertile soil,
the country was a huge grain producer 

00:02:09.015 --> 00:02:11.360
especially in these regions.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.057
Over time, it became known as 
the breadbasket of the Soviet Union

00:02:16.057 --> 00:02:20.510
and Joseph Stalin wanted 
complete control of it.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:24.000
When Stalin rose to power in the mid-1920s

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:29.044
a distinctly Ukrainian culture and 
national identity were thriving.

00:02:29.044 --> 00:02:32.830
But by the late-1920s, he and other Soviet leaders

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.808
feared it could bring on a Ukrainian revolution.

00:02:35.808 --> 00:02:42.000
They decided to crackdown on what they saw
as an ideological threat to the Soviet regime

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.280
and they began a widespread, violent purge
of Ukrainian intellectuals

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.791
along with priests, and religious structures.

00:02:50.791 --> 00:02:53.195
You have to think about it as kind of 

00:02:53.195 --> 00:02:57.720
decapitating the leadership of the country.

00:02:57.720 --> 00:02:59.813
You cut off its head, basically.

00:02:59.813 --> 00:03:03.738
Around the same time, Stalin introduced a
“Five Year Plan”

00:03:03.738 --> 00:03:08.026
that would eventually give him control of
Ukrainian agriculture.

00:03:08.026 --> 00:03:13.090
The goal was to industrialize all of 
the Soviet Union at a rapid pace

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:17.663
which meant building up industries like 
electricity, coal, and steel.

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:23.204
To fund this project, Stalin turned to the
“collectivization” of agriculture.

00:03:23.204 --> 00:03:26.442
Which meant consolidating 
individual farms

00:03:26.442 --> 00:03:31.584
across all of the Soviet Union 
into large, state run farms.

00:03:31.584 --> 00:03:37.350
If you combine, you know, 60, 70, 80 
small farms into one big farm

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.430
it's easier to control it.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:47.043
It's easier to extract surplus grain or surplus
sugar or whatever that farm produces.

00:03:47.043 --> 00:03:51.980
In Ukraine, the plan gave Stalin direct control
over grain production

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.930
which meant he could extract all of the crop
to sell to the West

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.338
as a way to fund Soviet industrialization.

00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:05.370
By this policy of industrialization, by taking
away everything that grew on the land

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.923
Stalin aimed to really destroy that connection
between the land and life itself.

00:04:10.923 --> 00:04:15.418
That was one of the key ideological things
in industrialization

00:04:15.418 --> 00:04:18.607
saying that it's not about the land, 
it's about the state

00:04:18.607 --> 00:04:22.568
the Soviet state that will then 
provide for your livelihood.

00:04:22.568 --> 00:04:26.745
Many Ukrainian farmers who had worked 
independently for their entire lives 

00:04:26.745 --> 00:04:28.514
resisted Stalin’s plan.

00:04:28.514 --> 00:04:31.464
So, Stalin found another way to attack them.

00:04:31.464 --> 00:04:34.933
He launched a propaganda campaign 
to smear farmers.

00:04:34.933 --> 00:04:39.139
He labeled anyone resistant 
to collectivization, a “kulak”

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:45.123
a Russian term for a wealthy peasant and depicted
them as greedy, exploiters, 

00:04:45.123 --> 00:04:47.520
and enemies of the state.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.900
And sometimes, as literal parasites.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.093
This is a way to drive a wedge within a community.

00:04:55.093 --> 00:05:01.612
And it's a way to also justify, I think, 
what the Soviet state

00:05:01.612 --> 00:05:06.527
and what the Communist Party is trying to do 
on the ideological side.

00:05:06.527 --> 00:05:12.599
No matter how rich or poor, Stalin seized
the belongings of the so-called “kulaks".

00:05:12.599 --> 00:05:17.764
He then exiled, imprisoned, or executed 
hundreds of thousands of them.

00:05:17.764 --> 00:05:24.557
And for the farmers who remained, 
he engineered a famine to starve them.

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:31.300
In 1931, Stalin deliberately set quotas 
for grain production

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:35.331
that were far beyond the capacity of farmers
across the Soviet Union.

00:05:35.331 --> 00:05:38.020
When farmers failed to meet those quotas

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:43.430
Stalin's men swept their farms to confiscate
all the grain they could find.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:50.537
Records show the Soviets took over 4 million
tons of grain from Ukraine alone in 1932.

00:05:50.537 --> 00:05:55.960
That same year, a new law punished anyone
who took even a handful of grain

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:01.989
or was caught hiding grain or bread — with
10 years in prison, or the death penalty.

00:06:01.989 --> 00:06:06.389
Stalin's oppressive collection policy created
a famine that started spreading

00:06:06.389 --> 00:06:10.145
in grain-producing regions across 
the Soviet Union.

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:13.710
Some party members sent Stalin letters 
about the growing crisis

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:15.770
pleading for a change in policy.

00:06:15.770 --> 00:06:18.347
And that's what makes it so diabolical because 

00:06:18.347 --> 00:06:23.870
if a government is really concerned
about its population, its people

00:06:23.870 --> 00:06:29.026
then at the end of the first famine year,
it can reverse its policies.

00:06:29.026 --> 00:06:32.552
But instead, the government and the party
actually doubled down.

00:06:32.552 --> 00:06:35.230
Their commitment to collectivization 

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:38.428
made the famine deadly in many parts 
of the Soviet Union.

00:06:38.428 --> 00:06:43.749
But when it came to Ukraine, Stalin's need
for the complete submission of its people

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:46.461
compounded the effects of the famine.

00:06:46.461 --> 00:06:53.349
In the fall and winter of 1932, Soviet police
began seizing not just grain

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:55.629
but anything edible.

00:06:55.629 --> 00:06:58.247
Even livestock.

00:06:58.247 --> 00:07:02.930
Farms in Ukraine and sometimes entire villages,
were “blacklisted”

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:09.861
for missing grain quotas, torn apart for food,
and prohibited from receiving any supplies.

00:07:09.861 --> 00:07:15.735
In January 1933, knowing Ukrainians were leaving
in search of food

00:07:15.735 --> 00:07:23.006
Stalin closed the borders of Ukraine and policed
migration from Ukrainian villages to cities, too.

00:07:23.006 --> 00:07:27.210
In the coming months, tens of thousands 
of Ukrainian villagers

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.999
were caught trying to flee

00:07:28.999 --> 00:07:33.004
and were sent back to their homes to starve.

00:07:33.004 --> 00:07:36.586
This was a targeted extermination of peasantry.

00:07:42.218 --> 00:07:48.330
People try to find food, you know, wherever
they could in rivers and streams.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:53.300
They began to eat their animals, their pets.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:59.199
They began to catch birds and mice, whatever
they could find on trees

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:01.333
that would give them some sort of sustenance.

00:08:01.333 --> 00:08:06.348
People were so desperate that they ate 
flesh from animals

00:08:06.348 --> 00:08:08.435
that they found on the road

00:08:08.435 --> 00:08:11.577
and some even resorted to cannibalism.

00:08:15.671 --> 00:08:21.410
But even in this unimaginable suffering, 
Ukrainians fought for their lives 

00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:23.516
and each other.

00:08:23.516 --> 00:08:25.919
She was my great grandmother.

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:29.729
I have fond memories of her.

00:08:29.729 --> 00:08:32.839
My dad took me to her village, 
I was really young.

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:35.390
So Lydia's house is actually my first memories.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:40.550
The way Holodomor happened, it was mostly
happening in the rural areas.

00:08:40.550 --> 00:08:42.536
Lydia was a student at the time.

00:08:42.536 --> 00:08:46.050
And she actually told me about how they survived.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:51.335
[in Ukrainian] At night, we dug a hole under a tree. 

00:08:51.335 --> 00:08:55.887
[in Ukrainian] We put a barrel in the hole 
and poured grain in there

00:08:55.887 --> 00:08:59.306
and covered the barrel with soil. 

00:08:59.306 --> 00:09:04.551
At night they would make flour out of that
wheat and make some makeshift bread.

00:09:04.551 --> 00:09:07.196
And the reason why they would do it at night
is that nobody could see

00:09:07.196 --> 00:09:09.294
the smoke coming out of the windows.

00:09:09.294 --> 00:09:11.810
Lydia describes horrific village scenes

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:17.371
seeing “a mother die of starvation” and
“police taking away children.”

00:09:17.371 --> 00:09:23.183
Once, when Lydia saw her neighbors who had
“bloated bodies and couldn’t get up”

00:09:23.183 --> 00:09:25.831
she started to sneak them “bread and milk".

00:09:25.831 --> 00:09:32.275
It’s likely due to her efforts, that seven
people, out of that family of nine, survived.

00:09:34.839 --> 00:09:42.572
After its peak in May and June of 1933, 
the famine slowly started subsiding.

00:09:42.572 --> 00:09:45.650
Likely because of a weakened labor force,
the Soviet regime

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:50.551
finally took measures to decrease 
grain confiscations and arrests.

00:09:50.551 --> 00:09:53.771
By 1934, most regions collectivized.

00:09:53.771 --> 00:09:57.736
Almost all farmers were working for the state.

00:09:57.736 --> 00:10:02.283
Though we will never know the total number
of deaths from the Holodomor

00:10:02.283 --> 00:10:07.099
a recent study estimated nearly 4 million
Ukrainians killed.

00:10:07.099 --> 00:10:12.994
Places like the North Caucasus, with a large
Ukrainian population, suffered greatly, too.

00:10:12.994 --> 00:10:18.966
And further east, Kazakhstan lost at least
a third of its population.

00:10:18.966 --> 00:10:21.920
So many people perished during the Holodomor

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:27.316
that the Soviet Union had to send people over
to Ukraine to rebuild the labor force.

00:10:27.316 --> 00:10:32.513
The main goal was to resettle those areas
in order to work the land.

00:10:32.513 --> 00:10:37.251
There were settlers brought in from various
parts of the Soviet Union 

00:10:37.251 --> 00:10:39.419
but mainly from Russia.

00:10:39.419 --> 00:10:44.187
This resettlement program set off a wave of
future campaigns over the years.

00:10:44.187 --> 00:10:46.935
Many Russians moved here in the east and south

00:10:46.935 --> 00:10:50.856
which are, to this day, places with 
large Russian populations.

00:10:53.123 --> 00:10:57.896
In Russia, Stalin carried out 
a massive disinformation campaign

00:10:57.896 --> 00:11:00.233
to cover up the famine he'd created.

00:11:00.233 --> 00:11:05.192
Throughout the crisis, he outright denied
that a famine ever took place.

00:11:05.192 --> 00:11:08.490
He banned the Soviet press 
from reporting on the famine

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:12.079
and banned foreign correspondents 
from even going to Ukraine.

00:11:12.079 --> 00:11:17.519
But, he strategically allowed language that
would effectively downplay the Holodomor

00:11:17.519 --> 00:11:21.629
words like “food shortages” or 
“food supply problems”.

00:11:21.629 --> 00:11:26.380
For example, Walter Duranty — a Moscow correspondent
for the New York Times

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:31.690
who won a Pulitzer for his reporting on 
Stalin’s success with the Five Year Plan

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:34.130
denied the famine in his reporting

00:11:34.130 --> 00:11:35.990
calling it a “food shortage".

00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:40.410
Duranty was challenged by others, including
journalist Gareth Jones

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:45.765
who snuck into Ukraine and wrote a series
of articles on how “famine ruled Russia".

00:11:45.765 --> 00:11:48.480
At the time, Russia was a term 
many journalists used

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:51.130
to describe the Soviet Union as a whole

00:11:51.130 --> 00:11:52.503
including Ukraine.

00:11:52.503 --> 00:11:56.990
Jones wrote: “Everywhere was the cry, 'there
is no bread.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:11:58.300
We are dying.'”

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:03.979
In response — Duranty, who had more influence
than Jones, put out another article

00:12:03.979 --> 00:12:09.475
this time insisting that “Russians were
hungry, but not starving".

00:12:09.475 --> 00:12:14.180
Several photographers who also tried to expose
the humanitarian crisis

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.673
were arrested for their photos.

00:12:16.673 --> 00:12:20.340
Some managed to get their images 
to leaders in the West

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:24.460
but the images didn’t mobilize support 
for Ukraine.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:28.449
The West didn’t want to get involved in
Soviet politics.

00:12:28.449 --> 00:12:35.460
The most cautionary tale is remembering that
the world didn't come to Ukraine's rescue.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.966
It seemed like an internal issue.

00:12:37.966 --> 00:12:41.533
There was a lot of misinformation, disinformation.

00:12:41.533 --> 00:12:45.910
Outside of controlling the media narrative
Stalin destroyed archives

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:49.830
and made sure death certificates didn’t
use the word “starvation” 

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:51.128
as a cause of death.

00:12:51.128 --> 00:12:55.060
And then there was the case of the 1937 census

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.839
whose findings showed a drop 
in the population of Ukraine.

00:12:58.839 --> 00:13:04.130
So, Stalin decided that he couldn't allow
this to be made public.

00:13:04.130 --> 00:13:06.949
The people who compiled the census

00:13:06.949 --> 00:13:10.839
most of them were arrested and 
some were even executed

00:13:10.839 --> 00:13:13.730
and the census never became public.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:16.154
Over the course of the next several decades

00:13:16.154 --> 00:13:19.993
the Soviet Union continued to suppress 
the Holodomor.

00:13:19.993 --> 00:13:26.114
But in Ukraine, the truth was quietly passed
down through generations.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.600
In more recent years, the Holodomor has been
recognized by more than a dozen countries

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:35.905
as a genocide.

00:13:35.905 --> 00:13:40.199
In fact Raphael Lemkin, the researcher who
coined the term genocide

00:13:40.199 --> 00:13:43.760
applied it to Ukraine on the basis of four reasons:

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.819
The extermination of intellectuals.

00:13:46.819 --> 00:13:49.459
The destruction of churches and priests

00:13:49.459 --> 00:13:52.104
The starvation of farmers.

00:13:52.104 --> 00:13:56.839
And the fragmentation of Ukrainian people
through resettlement.

00:13:57.434 --> 00:14:02.964
But the government of Russia has yet to acknowledge
any historical responsibility for the famine.

00:14:02.964 --> 00:14:08.237
They've maintained — falsely — that the
famine hit Russia just as hard.

00:14:08.237 --> 00:14:13.892
And today, similar tactics of disinformation
are being used to perpetrate 

00:14:13.892 --> 00:14:17.879
another violent attack against Ukrainians.

00:14:17.879 --> 00:14:21.709
If one looks at the coverage provided by 
Russian state media.

00:14:21.709 --> 00:14:24.472
It seems like the Ukrainians keep shelling
themselves.

00:14:24.472 --> 00:14:30.000
It seems like it's somehow the Ukrainians'
fault that there is this war in their own land.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.017
It's war against our people.

00:14:33.017 --> 00:14:36.327
I can't imagine, the second time we step
in the same river.

00:14:36.327 --> 00:14:41.203
Whole cities right now in Ukraine 
are being obliterated.

00:14:41.203 --> 00:14:47.000
It is really my fear that if Russia were to
be successful in this current invasion of

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:51.780
Ukraine they will make sure that this page
of history is rewritten and is forgotten.

